WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
It can be hard to make
rational sense of the world.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.300
But is your brain
your own worst enemy?

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.720
Here are four of the many ways
your brain's processing shortcuts

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.900
are playing tricks on you.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.900
Welcome to cognitive bias.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.900
Understanding a bit about it could
change the way you see the world.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.433
So here goes.

00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:23.900
A recent peer-reviewed scientific
study found caffeine consumption

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.500
is strongly linked
with developing cancer.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.000
On this scale,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.600
to what extent
do you agree or disagree

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.300
with the findings of this study?

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.300
Relax,

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:35.900
this study is fake.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.300
However, your answer
will be directly influenced

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.500
by the amount of caffeine you drink.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.500
Cognitive bias number one...

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Self-serving bias is your brain's

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.500
strong natural tendency to interpret
information in such a way as

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.300
to unduly favour itself.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.700
In this experiment,

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.900
caffeine drinkers rated the
study's validity consistently lower

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
than non-caffeine drinkers.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.500
Subjects with a negative personal
stake in the outcome of research

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.500
were less convinced by the data.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.600
How irrational.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.700
Your brain will reject
perfectly viable information

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.900
simply because
it has negative implications

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.000
for your personal beliefs
and behaviours.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.300
Likewise, it will tend
to eagerly accept information

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.900
with positive implications,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.600
even if that information
is flawed or inconclusive.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.500
So why does your brain do this?

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:32.300
Self-serving bias protects one's
fragile ego from threat and injury.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.800
That last group presentation
you gave was either a success,

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.600
thanks to your brilliant work.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.300
Or was a failure,
thanks to everyone else.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.600
You gotta look after that ego.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.300
OK, a new thought experiment.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.200
Look at this parking.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.500
What do you think
of the red car's driver?

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.200
If you thought poorly
of the driver's character,

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.000
you have performed
cognitive bias number two.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.400
This is your brain's attempt
to explain behaviour

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.500
by placing undue emphasis on internal
characteristics of the person,

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.700
rather than external factors.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.400
Consider that just moments ago,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.900
these cars were parked in a way

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.700
that left the red car's driver
with little option.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.000
Does that change your opinion?

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:30.500
Fundamental attribution error
is often performed when driving.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.100
I'm speeding because I'm in a rush,

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.400
whereas, they're speeding because
they're an inconsiderate maniac.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.300
Your brain has limited capacity
to interpret the world.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.300
It can observe the badly parked car

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.600
and understand
that someone put it there,

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:46.800
but that's it.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
To theorise about the historical
arrangement of the cars,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.100
or the situational
needs of the driver

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.800
is a complex and
potentially unending use

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.200
of finite cognitive resources.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.000
On to the next one.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.000
Here's a famous experiment
by Peter Wason.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.500
Play along at home.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.400
Subjects were given
a three number sequence,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.800
told that it follows a simple rule,
and asked to figure out the rule.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.300
They were allowed to suggest
their own number sequences

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.500
and told to continue
until they were confident

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.200
that they had cracked the rule.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.700
Were you thinking
of a sequence like this?

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:26.600
This follows the rule.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.100
And another, something like this?

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.300
This also follows the rule.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.400
So, what is the rule?

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.900
It's to multiply by two, right?

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.300
Well...no.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.600
Your brain just performed
another cognitive bias,

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.800
The actual rules is any sequence
of numbers in ascending order.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.700
So what went wrong?

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.600
Your brain landed on its first
hypothesis, multiply by two,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.400
from there, every suggested
number sequence was used

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.200
to confirm that initial hypothesis
rather than actually test it.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.300
A rational approach would be to
attempt to disprove this hypothesis

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.000
by suggesting other number
sequences that didn’t follow it.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:12.100
But, your brain isn't rational.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:17.900
It has a tendency to search for,
interpret, favour and recall

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.500
information that confirms
its pre-existing beliefs,

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:22.600
numbers or otherwise.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.700
Confirmation bias
is based on limitations

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.700
in the brain's ability
to handle complex tasks,

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.700
and the shortcuts
that it uses as a result.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.800
The brain finds it really difficult

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.200
to test alternative
hypotheses in parallel.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.400
It's good, but it's not that good.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:44.100
OK,

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.500
so you've learnt
a few cognitive biases,

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.400
you're now prepared to combat
them in your own brain.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.900
After all, knowing
is half the battle, right?

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.400
Well, not exactly.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.700
That's cognitive bias number four.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.700
The action figure
and TV character, G.I. Joe,

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.000
famously said,
"Knowing is half the battle."

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.500
When it comes to cognitive bias,
he was well out.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.700
Knowing is one thing,

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:18.000
but habits, situations and other
processes still rule the roost.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.300
Self-awareness wont beat it.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.800
You may know a badly parked car
does not make a bad person,

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.300
but you'll still feel negatively
towards them.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:31.200
You may know your brain will take
shortcuts to confirm the hypotheses

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:32.500
it already holds,

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.600
but it will still take
those shortcuts.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.100
You may know that your brain
will protect your ego at every turn,

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.900
but the ego security
will still be out in force.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:48.000
So knowing about cognitive biases
is way less than half the battle.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.100
Even knowing the G.I. Joe fallacy
about knowing about cognitive biases,

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.900
is still way less
than half the battle.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.600
Funny how your brain can pontificate
about its own limitations

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:03.500
but do almost nothing about them.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.900
But, in the true spirit
of cognitive bias

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.900
you will be able
to point it out in others.

